::GEO_U05_L06_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L06_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.7
Question:
A sector in diagram 1 has radius 6 and central angle 60°. Which expression gives its arc length?
{
=A. 60/360(2π6)#Arc length is the fraction of the circumference determined by the central angle: θ/360(2πr).
~B. 60/360(π6^2)#This is the sector area structure, not arc length.
~C. 2π6#This is the full circumference, not the arc for a 60° sector.
~D. π6^2#This is the full circle area, not an arc length.
}

::GEO_U05_L06_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L06_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.7
Question:
A sector in diagram 2 has radius 8 and central angle 90°. Which expression gives its sector area?
{
~A. 90/360(2π8)#This is the arc length structure, not sector area.
=B. 90/360(π8^2)#Sector area is the central-angle fraction of the circle area.
~C. 2π8#This is circumference, not area.
~D. 90π#This ignores the radius and the fraction of the circle.
}

::GEO_U05_L06_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L06_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.7
Question:
A 120° arc has radius 9. What coefficient of π gives the arc length?
{
~A. 9π#This uses the radius but not the central-angle fraction.
~B. 120π#This uses the angle as a length coefficient.
=C. 6π#Use 120/360(2π9), so the coefficient of π is 6.
~D. Cannot be determined#The radius and central angle are given, so the arc length coefficient can be determined.
}

::GEO_U05_L06_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L06_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.7
Question:
A 45° sector has radius 10. What coefficient of π gives the sector area?
{
~A. 10π#This uses the radius but does not square it or use the angle fraction.
~B. 45π#This uses the angle as an area coefficient.
~C. 2.5π#This coefficient comes from arc length, not sector area.
=D. 12.5π#Use 45/360(π10^2), so the coefficient of π is 12.5.
}

